 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy: 2Q (Two-Queue)
+
+The cache uses a 2Q policy with two LRU queues: **Am** (recently accessed once) and **A1** (reused entries).
+
+- **Am (first-use queue)**: New entries are admitted to the head of Am with their full JPEG size counted against the 12 GiB limit. Am receives entries on first admission and on eviction from A1. Am is evicted from the tail when space is needed.
+- **A1 (reuse queue)**: When an entry in Am is accessed a second time, it is promoted to the head of A1. A1 is evicted from the tail when space is needed and Am has no evictable entries.
+- **Queue split**: Am is capped at 25% of the 12 GiB budget (3 GiB). A1 occupies the remaining 75% (9 GiB). This split is a tunable parameter.
+- **Promotion**: A hit on an Am entry moves it to the head of A1 (demoting the tail of A1 if needed). A hit on an A1 entry moves it to the head of A1.
+- **Admission**: On cache miss, the completed sheet is admitted to Am head. If Am would exceed its budget, evict from Am tail until within budget. If Am is empty and space is still needed, evict from A1 tail.
+- **All eviction and promotion bookkeeping stays inside the preview worker**. The external interface, render queue concurrency (four jobs), and 12 GiB disk budget are unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (Two-Queue) with Am/A1 split at 25%/75% | LRU; Frequency-based with aging | 2Q protects comparison sheets (reused entries in A1) from being evicted by long sequential screening scans (one-use entries in Am). The extra bookkeeping (two queues, promotion logic, split parameter) is accepted to keep the working set warm during big screenings. LRU was rejected because a long scan evicts the comparison set. Frequency with aging was rejected because counters add maintenance and yesterday's favorites can linger too long after a project switch. |
+| Am budget fraction | 25% (3 GiB of 12 GiB) | 10%, 33%, 50% | 25% gives Am enough room for a screening's leading edge without starving A1. 10% risks promoting too aggressively; 50% weakens scan resistance. Tunable for future workload shifts. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Runtime tuning of the Am/A1 split based on observed hit-rate telemetry.
+2. Whether to persist queue state across worker restarts (currently cold-start on restart).
